{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for task/resident-100x at exact head 1b3df0ec4e035051ec625e23d414da8e8c1e99f9. Head and clean status verified before and after; probes deleted, worktree unchanged. Gates green on the pristine worktree: vet, build, test, -race. Disclosure: a delegate ran the sweep under my direction; I reproduced the blocker and the sharpest test gap myself, live, before signing.\n\nThe fold rewrite is correct and the performance claim is real. Decision equivalence was checked the only way that settles it: the real 350-event log, ref stepped commit by commit, sha256 of the projection JSON from this branch's incremental path compared against workroom.Fold built by main e5ee7a0 at all 351 prefixes — byte-identical, no differences. Complexity went from quadratic to linear on request-heavy histories (main 1.80/5.57/18.7/67.3 ms at 500/1000/2000/4000, about 3.6x per doubling; this head 1.11/2.14/4.70/9.29 ms, about 1.98x), and the dependents index that does it is decision-preserving. Re-measured like-for-like on the real log, the speedup is 16.6x at depth 25, 72.5x at 100, and 234x at 350, with main's warm arm equal to its cold arm confirming the arms are comparable. The claim reproduces and is understated at real depth. That is the good news and I want it on the record before the blocker.\n\nF1, BLOCKER. Eager application is not gated on the chain's own admission rule, so a resident serves a healthy projection over a chain no auditor accepts. kernel.go submit() parses the genesis descriptor but never checks that the signing key's public half is the SequencerPublicKey that descriptor names — scanHead and scanAfter both check it per commit — and app.go acceptSnapshot applies the record on the strength of the CAS having succeeded, after which the next Snapshot short-circuits on head equality and the reader is never asked. Main had no such hole because every Snapshot was a full audit. I reproduced this against a live resident on both sides rather than in a unit fixture. Rotate .git/gitseq/sequencer under a running gs serve, leaving the descriptor untouched, then submit through the server. On main e5ee7a0 the resident refuses immediately: /v0/status returns the signature error and serves nothing. On this head the resident answers depth 2 with both decisions effective, while gs verify on the same repository rejects the very commit it is serving, and the ref has already advanced irreversibly. Close it by comparing the signing key against desc.SequencerPublicKey before the first SignedCommit, or by verifying the fresh commit before UpdateRef — either is O(1) per append — plus a test that rotates the key and asserts refusal.\n\nF2, MUST FIX. The delta path's sequencer-signature check is untested. I ran this mutation myself: delete the VerifySSHCommit call at kernel.go:608 and go test ./internal/kernel/ ./internal/app/ ./internal/workroom/ is entirely green. A resident reader would accept delta commits signed by any key and nothing in the suite would say so. TestReaderRejectsInvalidDeltaWithoutMutatingVerifiedCache only exercises altered causal trailers, which loadEvent catches first, so it never reaches the sequencer check. Close it by appending a commit signed by a non-descriptor key onto the verified frontier and asserting Reader.Load errors with reader.head and reader.log untouched.\n\nF3, MUST FIX. Two load-bearing acceptSnapshot guards are uncovered, both proven by mutation. Weakening the snapshotCache.Head != result.BaseHead check to the nil checks alone leaves the branch suite green, yet the failure is deterministic: an external append followed by a local Act with no intervening Snapshot makes the resident report depth 2 against a cold 3, and the external event is dropped from the projection permanently because the next Snapshot short-circuits on head equality. Deleting the result.Replay guard is likewise green. Both want a test that compares against a fresh Open(...).Snapshot(). For contrast, deleting the start reconciliation search in Snapshot IS caught by TestSnapshotCachesTheVerifiedHead — that test is real.\n\nF4, MUST FIX, methodology. The cited benchmarks cannot detect the regression they guard, and I confirmed the fixture by reading it. BenchmarkWarmSubmitAtResidentDepth20000 takes base from scanHead at genesis, then sets Verification.Depth and Events to 20000 and pads Dedup with 20000 entries whose Commit is the literal string \"synthetic\". The cache head stays at genesis and the real chain is 100 commits, so scanAfter starts at genesis and does the same git work as scanHead. Measured at matched counts the incremental arm is 1 to 3 percent slower, and making scanAfter delegate straight to scanHead moves the numbers by noise — the benchmark cannot see its own subject removed. The only route to a 100x figure from these fixtures is CatchUp/1 against ColdAudit/100, which is not like-for-like. That benchmark also never executes scanAfter at all, and BenchmarkWarmAppendToSnapshotAtResidentDepth20000 pairs a synthetic folder with a real-chain cache, a combination production never produces. Since the real speedup is larger than claimed, this costs nothing to fix properly: build a real chain at depth D and compare a cold Snapshot against a warm Snapshot after a one-commit advance, at several D.\n\nF5, SHOULD FIX. app.go record() passes Payload and Attachments into workroom.Record and foldState.records pins them for the process lifetime, but fold.go reads Payload once at append and reads Attachments never. The live workroom is only 293 KB over 350 events, but PayloadCeiling is 1 MiB and this is the resident design. Null them on the retained parsedRecord after Decode; the kernel already does exactly this via eventWithoutPayload.\n\nF6, informational and pre-existing. A cached Snapshot is shared mutable state across callers, verified identical on main e5ee7a0, so not a regression — and this branch closed the strictly worse variant, mutation reaching the resident fold, with cloneStringMap. In-tree consumers are read-only. Worth a doc note now that the snapshot is long-lived.\n\nWHAT HELD, and it is substantial. Eleven hostile delta shapes fed to a warm Reader — unsigned by sequencer, tree not matching signed intent, forged actor signature, substituted actor key, target naming another chain, altered trailers, truncated envelope, empty message, replayed dedup key, merge commit, wrong parent — were all rejected with reader.head, reader.target, Verification, every dedup entry and deltaScans unchanged after each, and the reader still correct afterwards. Rewind, re-advance along a discarded fork, and reset to genesis each force a full audit while a legitimate sibling advance still takes the delta path. Full and delta verification agree at all 31 prefixes of a stepped chain, and the concatenated delta stream equals the cold stream position for position. Under -race, six writers with immediate replays produce exactly 30 events with none duplicated or missing, and ten concurrent local acts against six external plus a concurrent Snapshot poller leave the resident projection byte-identical to cold. Poisoning attempts through the returned Projection and through the returned Submission.Record both fail. Out-of-order acceptSnapshot is a no-op via the BaseHead gate. scanAfter's additions map merges only after every commit verifies, so no failure path mutates trusted state. The blocker is one missing check on one path, not a flaw in the design."}